---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout title={`Legal Fundamentals | ${SITE_TITLE}`} description="Analysis of the Bolam test, Bolitho qualification, CPR Part 35 compliance, and breach of duty vs causation in radiological reporting.">
	<PageHero
		eyebrow="Legal Fundamentals"
		title="The Legal Architecture of Radiological Negligence"
		description="A structured analysis of how radiological interpretation is evaluated in English law — from the Bolam standard through Bolitho to modern CPR Part 35 requirements."
	/>

	<section class="bg-white py-16 dark:bg-navy-900">
		<div class="container-page space-y-12">
			<!-- Bolam Test -->
			<Section eyebrow="The Bolam Standard" title="What standard of care does the law impose on radiologists?">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					The <em>Bolam</em> test remains the single most important legal standard in clinical negligence law in England and Wales. It was articulated by McNair J in <em>Bolam v Friern Hospital Management Committee</em> [1957] 1 WLR 582:
				</p>

				<blockquote class="mt-6 border-l-4 border-teal-500 bg-teal-50 p-5 dark:border-teal-400 dark:bg-teal-950/30">
					<p class="italic text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-200">
						"The [defendant] must be supported by a responsible body of medical men skilled in that particular art… where there is a divergency of leading opinion one or other or both can be considered not negligent."
					</p>
				</blockquote>

				<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					In the context of radiological interpretation, <strong>Bolam</strong> means that a radiologist's opinion is not negligent if a responsible body of radiologists would have reached the same conclusion on the same images. The test is <em>doctor-centric</em>: it asks what the profession accepts as proper practice, not what a layperson might consider reasonable.
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
					<div class="card p-6 border-l-4 border-l-teal-500">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Application to Diagnostic Imaging</h3>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The Bolam standard is most protective when applied to areas of genuine disagreement within the profession. Where there is a responsible body of radiologists who would interpret an image as the defendant did, the Bolam test will protect that radiologist even if another responsible body would have reached a different conclusion.
						</p>
						<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								Subtle findings below the threshold of routine detection
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								Image quality limitations (motion artefact, beam hardening)
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								Protocol-specific diagnostic sensitivity
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-teal-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg>
								Confidence levels and differential diagnoses
							</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6 border-l-4 border-l-amber-400">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Limitations in Claimant Cases</h3>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Claimant counsel will often argue that the Bolam defence fails where there is a single dominant standard of care — i.e. where any competent radiologist would have seen the finding. This is the "no divergence" argument: if expert consensus is overwhelming, Bolam provides no shield.
						</p>
						<ul class="mt-4 space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-amber-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
								Where RCR Standards define a clear protocol standard
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-amber-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
								Where a finding is large, obvious, or unequivocal
							</li>
							<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
								<svg class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 text-amber-500" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 8v4M12 16h.01"/></svg>
								Where the radiologist deviated from their own trust's protocol
							</li>
						</ul>
					</div>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Bolitho Qualification -->
			<Section eyebrow="The Bolitho Qualification" title="When the court can reject even a responsible body of opinion">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					In <em>Bolitho v City and Hackham Health Authority</em> [1997] UKHL 46, the House of Lords clarified that a body of professional opinion must be <strong>logically supportable</strong>. Lord Browne-Wilkinson stated:
				</p>

				<blockquote class="mt-6 border-l-4 border-teal-500 bg-teal-50 p-5 dark:border-teal-400 dark:bg-teal-950/30">
					<p class="italic text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-200">
						"Before a court will accept that an opinion… is reasonable… the defendant has to demonstrate that the opinion had a logical basis."
					</p>
				</blockquote>

				<p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					This is the most powerful weapon in a claimant's armoury. In practice, a Bolitho challenge can succeed when:
				</p>

				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
					<div class="rounded-xl bg-navy-50 p-5 dark:bg-navy-950">
						<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">No logical basis</p>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The expert opinion cannot be reconciled with known radiological facts (e.g. claiming a finding was "not visible" when it occupied 15% of the screen).
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rounded-xl bg-navy-50 p-5 dark:bg-navy-950">
						<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Ignores established science</p>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The defence expert's opinion contradicts established physics, biology, or well-documented imaging literature.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rounded-xl bg-navy-50 p-5 dark:bg-navy-950">
						<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Contradicts the evidence</p>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The defence expert's conclusion is incompatible with what the images themselves demonstrate on re-derivation.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- CPR Part 35 -->
			<Section eyebrow="Procedural Compliance" title="CPR Part 35 and Form 35 requirements">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					CPR Part 35 is the procedural framework governing expert evidence in civil litigation. The key provisions for medico-legal radiology experts:
				</p>

				<div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
					<div class="card p-5">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">CPR 35.3 — Duty to the Court</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							An expert's overriding duty is to the court, not to the instructing party. This means the expert must present balanced, impartial analysis — acknowledging both the strengths and weaknesses of the case from a radiological perspective. The report should not be a partisan document.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card p-5">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">CPR 35.10 — Single Joint Expert</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The court may direct that a single joint expert be instructed. In complex radiological cases involving multiple imaging studies, the court often prefers a single independent expert to avoid conflicting expert evidence. Both sides' questions are submitted to the single expert.
						</p>
					</div>

					<div class="card p-5">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Form 35 — Content of Expert Reports</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							A Part 35 report must include: statement of truth, qualifications, qualifications to act as expert, facts relied on, literature/reference material, qualifications of any delegate used, and a clear distinction between facts, observations, and opinions. For radiological cases, this includes a tabulated protocol analysis and annotated images where appropriate.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Breach vs Causation -->
			<Section eyebrow="Causation Analysis" title="Breach of Duty vs. Material Contribution to Harm">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					Even where a breach of duty is established, the claimant must prove that the breach <strong>caused</strong> or <strong>materially contributed</strong> to the adverse outcome. This second element is often the more challenging one in radiological cases.
				</p>

				<div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">The "But For" Test</h3>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The basic causation test asks: "but for the defendant's breach, would the claimant have suffered the harm?" In radiological delay cases, this becomes: "but for the missed diagnosis, would the patient have had a materially different outcome?"
						</p>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The answer depends heavily on: the tumour biology (growth rate, grade, molecular profile), the treatment window available with earlier diagnosis, and the evidence of what actually happened in the claimant's care pathway.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Natural Disease Progression</h3>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The defendant will argue that the adverse outcome was caused by the natural progression of the disease, not by the radiologist's error. If the disease was already at an advanced stage at the time of the missed diagnosis, the delay may be considered clinically insignificant.
						</p>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							A common metric is the "treatment window" — the time between when the diagnosis should have been made and when it actually was. If the treatment window is shorter than the time required to achieve a better outcome, causation is difficult to establish.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<div class="mt-8 rounded-xl border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50/60 p-6 dark:border-amber-600/30 dark:bg-amber-950/20">
					<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200">Key Principle from <em>Hotson v East Berkshire HA</em> [1987]</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
						If the claimant had a less than 50% chance of a better outcome even with timely treatment, the claim will fail in its entirety. Causation is all-or-nothing at this threshold. The loss of a chance doctrine has not been accepted in personal injury cases in England and Wales.
					</p>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Textbook foundations: consent and autonomy -->
			<Section eyebrow="Textbook Foundations" title="Autonomy and consent — why the form is not the consent">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					The imaging-law literature treats consent as an exercise in autonomy rather than administration.
					In <em>Ethical and Legal Issues for Imaging Professionals</em>, Towsley-Cook and Young define
					autonomy as the principle that a person must not be treated without informed consent, and treat the
					consent form itself as important but limited — evidence of a process, never a substitute for it.
				</p>
				<blockquote class="mt-6 border-l-4 border-teal-500 bg-teal-50 p-5 dark:border-teal-400 dark:bg-teal-950/30">
					<p class="italic text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-200">
						"Informed consent is the written assent of a patient to receive a proposed treatment; adequate
						information is essential for the patient to give truly informed consent."
					</p>
					<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
						Towsley-Cook &amp; Young, <em>Ethical and Legal Issues for Imaging Professionals</em>, ch. 4
					</p>
				</blockquote>
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					Two features of that chapter transfer directly to English practice. First, the treatment of
					<strong>competence and surrogacy</strong>: where a patient cannot make the decision, consent passes
					to a surrogate or a court-appointed decision-maker. In England and Wales that question is now
					governed by the Mental Capacity Act 2005 — the presumption of capacity in s.1, the best-interests
					checklist in s.4, and the requirement to prefer the least restrictive option. Second, the discussion
					of <strong>therapeutic privilege</strong>, the withholding of information said to be harmful to the
					patient. The textbook presents it as a narrow exception requiring justification; English law does
					the same, and <em>Montgomery</em> confirms that the starting point is the material risks a
					reasonable patient in that patient's position would want to know.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50/60 p-6 dark:border-amber-600/30 dark:bg-amber-950/20">
					<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200">Jurisdiction note</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
						The consent chapter is written against United States law, including the Patient Self-Determination
						Act and state advance-directive regimes. Its ethical analysis transfers to UK practice, but the
						legal tests do not: the operative English authorities are <em>Montgomery v Lanarkshire Health
						Board</em> [2015] UKSC 11 on materiality of risk, the Mental Capacity Act 2005 on capacity and
						surrogacy, and the GMC's consent guidance on what must be discussed and recorded.
					</p>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Textbook foundations: confidentiality and disclosure -->
			<Section eyebrow="Textbook Foundations" title="Truthfulness, confidentiality and what must be disclosed">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					The companion chapter of the same textbook examines truthfulness and confidentiality. It analyses
					confidentiality through the idea of the <strong>obligatory secret</strong> — knowledge a person has a
					right or an obligation to conceal because harm would follow from its revelation — and identifies
					three kinds:
				</p>
				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
					<div class="rounded-xl bg-navy-50 p-5 dark:bg-navy-950">
						<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Natural</p>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							A secret that by its nature would be harmful if revealed.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rounded-xl bg-navy-50 p-5 dark:bg-navy-950">
						<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Promised</p>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							A secret the recipient has promised to conceal.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="rounded-xl bg-navy-50 p-5 dark:bg-navy-950">
						<p class="text-sm font-semibold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Professional</p>
						<p class="mt-2 text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							A secret maintained to protect the patient, society and the profession — treated as the most
							binding of the three.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					The reasoning behind that hierarchy transfers directly. The argument is that breach of the
					professional secret harms the patient, the profession and the community that depends on it, because
					patients who lose confidence may avoid necessary imaging altogether. That is the same policy that
					underpins confidentiality obligations in this jurisdiction.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-6 space-y-4">
					<div class="card p-5">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">The duty of candour</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Confidentiality is not a licence for concealment. Where a notifiable safety incident has
							occurred, the duty of candour in the Health and Social Care Act 2008 (Regulated Activities)
							Regulations 2014 requires an apology, a factual account of what happened, and an explanation
							of what will be done about it. Silence in the face of a complication is itself a breach.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-5">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Disclosure in the public interest</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							Confidentiality is not absolute. In <em>W v Egdell</em> [1990] Ch 359 the court recognised
							that a professional may be justified in disclosing information where there is a real risk of
							serious harm to others. The test is proportionality between the risk of disclosure and the
							risk of silence — the same balance the textbook describes when it sets out the exceptions to
							confidentiality and the duty to warn third parties.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-5">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">Anonymisation in expert work</h3>
						<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							For the medicolegal expert this chapter frames a practical duty. Source imaging carries
							personal data, so material reproduced in a report or a case discussion must be anonymised,
							and the lawful basis, scope of disclosure and retention period should be documented. It also
							explains why the RCR standards require clinical details to form part of the report: a future
							reviewer needs to see what the reporter actually knew.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
			</Section>

			<!-- Textbook foundations: provider liability -->
			<Section eyebrow="Textbook Foundations" title="Provider liability and the systems question behind an individual report">
				<p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
					The risk-management chapter of <em>Medicolegal Issues for Diagnostic Imaging Professionals</em>
					examines liability at three levels: the practitioner, the employer and the institution. Its central
					illustration is the doctrine of corporate negligence developed in <em>Darling v Charleston Community
					Hospital</em>, where the court held that a hospital owed its patient a duty of care which included
					protecting the patient from acts of negligence by its staff physicians where it
					"knows, or has reason to know, or should have known such acts were likely to occur" — concluding that
					the hospital was corporately responsible for the conduct of its medical staff and was expected to have
					policies and procedures reasonably designed to assure proper patient care.
				</p>
				<div class="mt-6 grid gap-6 lg:grid-cols-2">
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">The record-keeping lesson</h3>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							The chapter also recounts litigation in which a treating doctor's note that the patient "had
							done extremely well, no complaints" sat alongside nursing entries recording that the patient
							"had to be helped to the bathroom, complains of pain continuously, left leg felt paralyzed".
							Liability followed — for the acts themselves and for the institutional failure around them.
							The radiological analogue is the report and the request form: contradictory, cursory or
							hedged documentation is the raw material from which breach and causation are argued.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="card p-6">
						<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-navy-900 dark:text-slate-100">The UK translation</h3>
						<p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
							English law reaches a comparable destination by a different route. An employer is
							vicariously liable for the negligence of employees acting in the course of their employment,
							and the modern test looks to the closeness of the connection between the employment and the
							wrong (<em>Various Claimants v Catholic Child Welfare Society</em> [2012] UKSC 56 and the
							cases that follow it). The provider also owes duties of its own: the RCR standards require the
							employer to ensure software and hardware are available and maintained, to train staff on any
							locally deployed AI, and to put safeguards in place where private teleradiologists may be
							unfamiliar with local care pathways.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="mt-6 rounded-xl border border-amber-300 bg-amber-50/60 p-6 dark:border-amber-600/30 dark:bg-amber-950/20">
					<h3 class="text-base font-bold text-amber-900 dark:text-amber-200">Two distinctions that matter when pleading</h3>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300">
						<strong>First</strong>, the individual reporter and the provider are separate potential
						defendants, and a systems failure does not exonerate the reporter — nor does a defensible
						individual reading defeat a claim about how the service was configured. <strong>Second</strong>,
						the textbook's discussion of Good Samaritan statutes has no direct UK analogue: there is no
						statutory immunity for emergency assistance in England and Wales, and liability for emergency aid
						is determined by ordinary negligence principles. Comparative material of this kind should be used
						for its framework and ethical reasoning, not as authority on the applicable law.
					</p>
				</div>
				<p class="mt-6 text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400">
					Sources informing this section: Towsley-Cook &amp; Young, <em>Ethical and Legal Issues for Imaging
					Professionals</em> (chapters 4 and 5); Parelli (ed.), <em>Medicolegal Issues for Diagnostic Imaging
					Professionals</em> (chapters 5 and 11); Royal College of Radiologists, <em>Standards for
					interpretation and reporting of imaging investigations</em>; Halligan S, et al., <em>Clinical
					Radiology</em> 2026;92:1071622.
				</p>
			</Section>

			<!-- Summary takeaway -->
			<div class="rounded-2xl bg-gradient-to-br from-navy-900 to-navy-800 p-8 text-white">
				<h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">Key Takeaways for the Legal Practitioner</h3>
				<ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-200">
					<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
						<span class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-teal-500/30 text-center text-[0.6rem] font-bold text-teal-300">1</span>
						The Bolam test protects radiologists when a responsible body of peers would have reached the same conclusion — but only when there is genuine professional disagreement.
					</li>
					<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
						<span class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-teal-500/30 text-center text-[0.6rem] font-bold text-teal-300">2</span>
						Bolitho provides a powerful challenge to expert evidence that is not logically supportable — particularly where the defence opinion contradicts the images themselves.
					</li>
					<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
						<span class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-teal-500/30 text-center text-[0.6rem] font-bold text-teal-300">3</span>
						CPR Part 35 reports must be impartial and follow the prescribed format. Deviations from the standard format can undermine the weight given to expert evidence.
					</li>
					<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
						<span class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-teal-500/30 text-center text-[0.6rem] font-bold text-teal-300">4</span>
						Causation analysis is separate from breach analysis. A radiologist may be in breach of duty yet not liable if the delay was not causally significant.
					</li>
					<li class="flex items-start gap-2">
						<span class="mt-0.5 h-4 w-4 shrink-0 rounded-full bg-teal-500/30 text-center text-[0.6rem] font-bold text-teal-300">5</span>
						The expert witness must distinguish between the radiological analysis (demonstrability on the images) and the legal analysis (whether the failure met the Bolam standard).
					</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
</BaseLayout>
